---
import PuzzleFrame from '../../components/PuzzleFrame.astro';
import business from '../../data/puzzle-business.json';
const businessHub = Object.keys(import.meta.glob('../ues/business.astro')).length ? '/ues/business' : '/ues';
---
<PuzzleFrame title="Could a puzzle become a business?" description="An open puzzle business lab: five models, illustrative ranges, contribution and cash-recovery calculators, pilot gates and educational cases." active="business">
  <header class="p-section"><p class="eyebrow">POINTCAST × UES BUSINESS STUDIES · AN EDUCATIONAL CASE</p><h1>Make a picture.<br/>Build a business?</h1><p class="p-lead">A beautiful image is a beginning. A viable puzzle also needs a customer, a reliable cut, a workable cost, and a way to arrive with every piece.</p><div class="p-actions"><a class="p-button" href="#calculator">Try the numbers ↓</a><a class="p-button secondary" href="/puzzles/studio/">Take the design studio →</a></div><p class="p-note">All economics below are illustrative USD assumptions for a small US operation, not supplier quotes or observed PointCast results. PointCast has no puzzle inventory, sales forecast, factory commitment or investment offering.</p></header>
  <section class="p-section" id="models"><div class="p-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">01 · FIVE ROUTES TO MARKET</p><h2>Choose the<br/>operating model.</h2></div><p>Do not combine five businesses on day one. Match one customer and occasion to one channel, then earn the right to expand.</p></div><div class="p-grid"><article class="p-card"><p class="eyebrow">MODEL 01 · SMALL PILOT</p><h3>Make to learn</h3><p>One image, a few physical samples, then an optional 20–100-unit test only after the proof and demand study pass. Small runs limit stranded inventory but usually cost more per unit.</p><p><b>Test:</b> What image, size and difficulty do people choose? Would they pay the proposed price after seeing a real sample?</p><p><b>Watch:</b> Sample charges, freight, packaging differences, waste, missing-piece replacements and cash paid before sales.</p></article><article class="p-card"><p class="eyebrow">MODEL 02 · DIRECT RETAIL</p><h3>Own the shelf</h3><p>Sell a boxed edition at an assumed $28–40 plus explicitly disclosed shipping. Fulfillment, support and customer acquisition belong in the calculation.</p><p><b>Test:</b> Contribution per order after shipping and returns, not just a markup on factory cost.</p><p><b>Watch:</b> Card fees, chargebacks, tax setup, delivery promises, storage, damaged boxes and seasonal demand.</p></article><article class="p-card"><p class="eyebrow">MODEL 03 · WHOLESALE</p><h3>Reach another shop</h3><p>Illustrative $32 MSRP and $16 wholesale receipt. With $8–13 variable cost, contribution is $3–8 before overhead and any sales commission. Retailer terms must be negotiated.</p><p><b>Test:</b> Reorders and on-time, damage-free delivery, with a clear minimum order.</p><p><b>Watch:</b> Net payment terms, returns, display packaging, case packs, retailer margin and inventory credit risk.</p></article><article class="p-card"><p class="eyebrow">MODEL 04 · LICENSING</p><h3>License the picture</h3><p>An established publisher produces and sells under a negotiated art license. An illustrative 5–10% royalty on net receipts at $16 produces $0.80–1.60 per unit; this is a sensitivity range, not an industry contract benchmark.</p><p><b>Test:</b> Territory, channels, term, exclusivity, approvals, royalty reporting and returns deductions.</p><p><b>Watch:</b> Rights ownership, minimum guarantees, audit terms, sublicensing and unsold stock after termination.</p></article><article class="p-card"><p class="eyebrow">MODEL 05 · SUBSCRIPTION</p><h3>A puzzle every season</h3><p>A quarterly edition at an assumed $35–45, with shipping included or separately stated. Launch only after a repeatable product and fulfillment record exist.</p><p><b>Test:</b> Renewal intention, pause/cancel experience, churn and delivered contribution per shipment.</p><p><b>Watch:</b> Acquisition payback, automatic-renewal rules, failed payments, artwork cadence and inventory forecasting.</p></article><article class="p-card"><p class="eyebrow">CROSS-CHANNEL QUESTION</p><h3>One picture.<br/>Several lives.</h3><p>Buildworks can explore complementary small objects in its own studio. This puzzle case concentrates on a tactile picture, its assembly experience and its box.</p><p>Shared artwork would require an explicit license, consistent credits and a sensible product sequence. A bundle is an experiment, not assumed demand.</p></article></div></section>
  <section class="p-section" id="ranges"><div class="p-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">02 · COSTS ARE A RANGE</p><h2>Replace assumptions<br/>with evidence.</h2></div><p>These planning bands are deliberately broad. A dated quote for the selected image, count, packaging and destination replaces them before committing cash.</p></div><div class="p-table-wrap"><table class="p-table"><caption>Illustrative planning ranges · USD · no taxes or financing included</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Line</th><th scope="col">Planning range</th><th scope="col">What changes it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Landed puzzle, box & insert</th><td>$8–18 / unit at a planned run</td><td>Quantity, board, trim, die/tooling, printed box, freight and duties; small one-off samples may exceed this.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Mailer + packing materials</th><td>$0.75–2.00 / order</td><td>Box dimensions, damage protection, recyclable material choice and case quantity.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Handling labor</th><td>$1.50–4.00 / order</td><td>Paid packing time, picking, labels, support burden and fulfillment partner minimums.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">US outbound postage</th><td>$6–12 / order</td><td>Actual weight, dimensions, zone, service and negotiated rates; international is separate.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Artist fee / royalty</th><td>$0–3.00 / unit plus fixed art costs</td><td>Rights, human design work, license structure and volume. Zero does not mean art takes no labor.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Damage / replacement reserve</th><td>$0.50–2.00 / order</td><td>Missing pieces, transit damage, exchanges, return postage and refund rates.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Paid customer acquisition</th><td>$0–10 / order</td><td>Channel, conversion, repeat purchase and creative costs. Organic discovery still takes time.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Fixed launch work</th><td>$2,000–8,000</td><td>Design, proof rounds, testing, tooling, setup, photography and contingency; rent, tax, owner pay and financing are additional.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="p-small" style="margin-top:20px">The $9.50 landed default below is a hypothetical target for a planned run. It is not the currently observed price of a 100-unit batch. <a href="/puzzles/make/#suppliers">Compare actual dated supplier observations</a>.</p></section>
  <section class="p-section" id="calculator"><div class="p-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">03 · THE CONTRIBUTION TABLE</p><h2>Change a number.<br/>Watch the model.</h2></div><p>A single-item domestic order, charged in USD. Sales tax is excluded and assumed passed through; real fees may apply to tax too. Customer shipping is revenue, while actual postage is a cost. No information is sent or saved.</p></div><form id="p-economics"><fieldset id="p-economics-fields" disabled style="border:0;padding:0;margin:0"><div class="p-inputs"><label>Puzzle price ($)<input name="price" type="number" min="0" max="10000" step=".01" value="32" required/></label><label>Shipping charged to buyer ($)<input name="shippingCharge" type="number" min="0" max="10000" step=".01" value="7" required/></label><label>Landed puzzle, box & insert ($)<input name="landed" type="number" min="0" max="10000" step=".01" value="9.50" required/></label><label>Mailer / materials ($)<input name="mailer" type="number" min="0" max="10000" step=".01" value="1.25" required/></label><label>Handling labor ($)<input name="handling" type="number" min="0" max="10000" step=".01" value="2.50" required/></label><label>Outbound postage ($)<input name="postage" type="number" min="0" max="10000" step=".01" value="8" required/></label><label>Artist royalty per unit ($)<input name="royalty" type="number" min="0" max="10000" step=".01" value="1.50" required/></label><label>Replacement reserve ($)<input name="reserve" type="number" min="0" max="10000" step=".01" value="1.25" required/></label><label>Card fee (%)<input name="feePercent" type="number" min="0" max="100" step=".01" value="2.90" required/></label><label>Card fee fixed ($)<input name="feeFlat" type="number" min="0" max="10000" step=".01" value=".30" required/></label><label>Acquisition per order ($)<input name="cac" type="number" min="0" max="10000" step=".01" value="0" required/></label><label>Fixed launch costs ($)<input name="fixed" type="number" min="0" max="10000000" step="1" value="4500" required/></label><label>Prepaid inventory units<input name="lot" type="number" min="1" max="1000000" step="1" value="500" required/></label></div><div class="p-actions"><button type="submit" class="p-button">Calculate</button><button type="reset" class="p-button secondary">Reset assumptions</button></div></fieldset></form><div class="p-output" id="p-economics-output" role="status" aria-live="polite"><strong>$13.57 contribution / order</strong><p>$39.00 collected − $25.43 variable costs. $4,500 fixed launch work breaks even at 332 sales. This does not include owner pay, rent, taxes or financing.</p><p>A prepaid 500-unit lot plus fixed work needs $9,250 upfront. Simplified recovery of that prepaid cash takes 401 sales when each sale’s already-paid inventory cost is added back.</p></div><p class="p-small">Default payment fee: Stripe’s published 2.9% + 30¢ for US domestic cards, checked October 3, 2026. Actual merchant, card, refund, currency and tax treatment can differ. <a href="https://stripe.com/pricing" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Stripe pricing ↗</a></p><h3 style="margin-top:32px">Sensitivity at these assumptions</h3><div class="p-table-wrap"><table class="p-table"><caption>Change one variable from the current inputs</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Case</th><th scope="col">Contribution / order</th><th scope="col">Sales to cover fixed work</th></tr></thead><tbody id="p-sensitivity"><tr><th scope="row">Current inputs</th><td>$13.57</td><td>332</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Add $5 acquisition cost</th><td>$8.57</td><td>526</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Add $4 landed cost</th><td>$9.57</td><td>471</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Free customer shipping</th><td>$6.77</td><td>665</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="p-note" style="margin-top:22px">Unit break-even is not cash recovery. Prepaid stock ties up cash before any sale, and a slow sell-through can leave a profitable-per-unit project short of money. Cash recovery here assumes steady costs, no added fixed spending and no new inventory purchase. This is a teaching model.</p><noscript><p>The default worked example is visible. Enable JavaScript to calculate with different inputs.</p></noscript></section>
  <section class="p-section" id="pilot"><div class="p-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">04 · A SMALL PILOT WITH REAL GATES</p><h2>Earn the<br/>next batch.</h2></div><p>Start with Tidepool atlas or Saturday market, and test Cloud observatory as a larger-piece alternative. A piece count and price on this page are proposals, not production decisions.</p></div><div class="p-grid"><article class="p-card"><h3>Gate 1 / Evidence</h3><p>Define one audience, purchase occasion and acceptable price range. Compare five real puzzles. Interview voluntary adults with permission; no names or testimonials are published by default.</p><p><b>Deliver:</b> One-page demand hypothesis, competitor table and uncertainty list.</p></article><article class="p-card"><h3>Gate 2 / Physical proof</h3><p>One supplier quote, one art master, one boxed sample. Check color, cut, fit, handling, glare, piece count, print reference and packaging strength.</p><p><b>Deliver:</b> Dated quote, proof checklist, rights record and corrections.</p></article><article class="p-card"><h3>Gate 3 / Playtest</h3><p>Try the actual cut with a small consenting group of adults with varied experience and access needs. Record confusing regions, false fits, fatigue and missing pieces. Participation and buying are separate choices.</p><p><b>Deliver:</b> Anonymous observations, not an invented completed study.</p></article><article class="p-card"><h3>Gate 4 / Economics</h3><p>Replace assumptions with a destination-specific landed cost, postage and paid labor. Add a reserve. Stress-test half the expected demand, higher damage, and supplier delay.</p><p><b>Deliver:</b> Contribution model, cash timing and a stop rule.</p></article><article class="p-card"><h3>Gate 5 / Retail readiness</h3><p>Only after approval: explicit price, delivery estimate, age suitability, return/replacement policy, batch traceability and fulfillment capacity.</p><p><b>Deliver:</b> Sample-based product page; no unsupported certification or sustainable-material claim.</p></article><article class="p-card"><h3>Gate 6 / Reorder</h3><p>Choose in advance how much sell-through, replacement rate and contribution justify a reorder. The thresholds are yours to test, not an assumed universal benchmark.</p><p><b>Deliver:</b> Actual order ledger and reasons to continue, revise or stop.</p></article></div></section>
  <section class="p-section"><div class="p-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">05 · OPERATING REALITY</p><h2>The picture<br/>has a supply chain.</h2></div><p>Business studies examine how decisions travel through design, production, distribution and care.</p></div><div class="p-grid"><article class="p-card"><h3>Shipping & returns</h3><p>Measure the packed parcel. Test transit damage. State dispatch promises you can support, refund/cancellation terms, and a missing-piece policy. Budget support labor and reverse logistics.</p><a href="https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/selling-internet-prompt-delivery-rules" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FTC delivery guidance ↗</a></article><article class="p-card"><h3>Material & waste</h3><p>Ask for board, adhesive, ink, coating and bag specifications. Evaluate print overruns, cutting waste, rejected stock and disposal. Verify certifications and avoid broad “eco-friendly” promises.</p><a href="https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/environmental-claims-summary-green-guides" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FTC Green Guides ↗</a></article><article class="p-card"><h3>Safety & testing</h3><p>Determine intended age and applicable rules before manufacturing. Small pieces, coatings, wood edges and packaging need the right review. “For adults” is not a shortcut around product-safety responsibilities.</p><a href="/puzzles/make/#safety">Read the safety workflow →</a></article></div></section>
  <section class="p-section" id="study"><div class="p-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">06 · A CASE FOR THE BUSINESS CONCENTRATION</p><h2>Study the system.<br/>Then make a brief.</h2></div><p>This is a proposed educational case in PointCast’s independent University of El Segundo project. It does not represent an accredited qualification, an enrolled cohort, a commercial partner or a completed student venture.</p></div><div class="p-grid"><article class="p-card"><h3>UES business studies</h3><p>Compare the puzzle case with local businesses: customer need, variable cost, inventory timing and contribution. Keep geography, dates and source limits attached to the evidence.</p><a href={businessHub}>Open UES business studies →</a></article><article class="p-card"><h3>The design school</h3><p>Translate a customer and piece plan into image zones, hierarchy, print specs, access tests and a proof. The visual and business decisions should fit each other.</p><a href="/puzzles/studio/">Open the puzzle design studio →</a></article><article class="p-card"><h3>Industry Next</h3><p>Use the existing PointCast-hosted Industry Next Communications Lab as a companion for clear briefs and responsible claims. A dedicated puzzle collaboration is proposed; no external partner commitment is claimed.</p><a href="/communications-lab/">Explore the Communications Lab →</a><p class="p-small" style="margin-top:14px"><a href="https://www.industrynext.xyz/editorial/the-wild-one-cent/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Industry Next: One cent, remembered ↗</a>. A related case for separating product function from customer demand. Apply that distinction to physical proofs and actual puzzle buyers.</p></article></div><p class="p-small" style="margin-top:25px">Business notebook prepared October 3, 2026. <a href="/puzzles.json">Download the structured case data</a> · <a href="/puzzles/#sources">Research and sources</a>.</p></section>
</PuzzleFrame>
<script>
  import {economics,DEFAULTS} from '../../lib/puzzle-economics.mjs';
  function setupEconomics(){const form=document.querySelector<HTMLFormElement>('#p-economics');if(!form||form.dataset.ready)return;form.dataset.ready='true';form.addEventListener('submit',e=>e.preventDefault());(form.querySelector('#p-economics-fields') as HTMLFieldSetElement).disabled=false;const output=document.querySelector('#p-economics-output')!;const body=document.querySelector('#p-sensitivity')!;const usd=(n:number)=>n.toLocaleString('en-US',{style:'currency',currency:'USD'});const calc=()=>{const input=Object.fromEntries(Object.keys(DEFAULTS).map(k=>[k,Number((form.elements.namedItem(k) as HTMLInputElement).value)]));if(!form.checkValidity()){output.textContent='Enter valid, non-negative values in every field; inventory units must be a whole number of at least 1.';body.replaceChildren();return;}const result=economics(input);if(!result.valid){output.textContent='Check the inputs before calculating.';body.replaceChildren();return;}output.replaceChildren();const strong=document.createElement('strong');strong.textContent=`${usd(result.contribution)} contribution / order`;output.append(strong);const note=document.createElement('p');note.textContent=`${usd(result.revenue)} collected − ${usd(result.variable)} variable costs. ${result.breakEven===null?'Contribution is zero or negative: there is no finite unit break-even.':`${usd(input.fixed)} fixed work breaks even at ${result.breakEven.toLocaleString()} sales.`} Owner pay, rent, taxes and financing are additional.`;output.append(note);const cash=document.createElement('p');cash.textContent=`Prepaid inventory plus fixed work needs ${usd(result.prepaid)} upfront. ${result.cashRecovery===null?'Cash per sale is zero or negative, so this model cannot recover that cash.':`Simplified cash recovery takes ${result.cashRecovery.toLocaleString()} sales.${result.cashRecovery>input.lot?' This exceeds the current inventory lot; more stock and additional cash would be needed.':''}`}`;output.append(cash);body.replaceChildren();const cases=[['Current inputs',input],['Add $5 acquisition cost',{...input,cac:input.cac+5}],['Add $4 landed cost',{...input,landed:input.landed+4}],['Free customer shipping',{...input,shippingCharge:0}]];cases.forEach(([label,values])=>{const r=economics(values);const row=document.createElement('tr');const th=document.createElement('th');th.scope='row';th.textContent=String(label);const c=document.createElement('td');c.textContent=usd(r.contribution);const b=document.createElement('td');b.textContent=r.breakEven===null?'No finite break-even':r.breakEven.toLocaleString();row.append(th,c,b);body.append(row);});};form.addEventListener('submit',e=>{e.preventDefault();calc();});form.addEventListener('input',calc);form.addEventListener('reset',()=>setTimeout(calc,0));calc();}
  setupEconomics();document.addEventListener('astro:page-load',setupEconomics);
</script>
